  "notes": "Jazzmatazz is the one place on this axis where the harmony is composed rather than sampled, and the research says so in as many words: \"8- and 16-bar forms rather than 2-bar loops — the one place in this lane where the harmony is composed.\" That is authored as `melody.structure` `long_arc` plus `phraseBars` weighted onto 8 and 16, which is the single biggest departure from the parent, and it is why `arrangement` is overridden at all: the extended instrumental solo section is a real structural feature, so the bridge is lengthened, given every melodic part, and run at near-full density. The second half is the tension the research names — a programmed kit kept *deliberately stiff* against live players — so `quantizeStrength` is 0.95 and `swing` is 0.52 while the bass is the loosest on the axis: live electric or upright playing genuine changes, `followRootsProb` 0.35 over a 22-semitone register with five passing tones. The ride wash from the live kit is the `percs` override. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high and says the live-band construction is documented at album level rather than at session level. The inferred parameters are the swing amount and the solo-section bar count; the research says \"extended instrumental solo sections (8–16 bars)\" and everything else in the arrangement block follows from that number. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 47 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 48 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
